        <body>
            <div xml:id="d143">

                <pb facs="0310" n="210" sameAs="frus:frus1880/pg210"/>

                <head>No. 143.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Seward to Mr. Evarts.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1880-01-15"><hi rendition="#italic">January</hi> 15, 1880</date>.
                        (Received March 15.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 561.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Having great confidence in the good
                    judgment of General Stahel, and knowing that his experience in the management of
                    mixed cases <pb facs="0311" n="211" xml:id="pg211"/> both in China and Japan has
                    given him practical acquaintance with the subject, I sent to him, unofficially,
                    copies of the memoranda on procedure in mixed cases between our people and
                    subjects of China, and asked his views. He has responded under date of December
                    10, and I have now the honor to place his letter before you.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>GEOEGE F. SEWAED.</signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d143-a1.xml" xpointer="d143-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
